A||N||1||Vlad||Removing dead a/c compressor.||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||01-31-2001||09:29 PM||||Hello,<P>Since I got my car, A/C hasn't work. Well... No magic. Today I discovered that compressor is held by one bolt only. According to Haynes, there have to be three bolts. One on them just missing, the second one is gone and<BR>bolt hole in both, bracket and compressor, is broken. The compressor is dislocated from its normal position and its belt has a huge slack. <BR>Since I don't need a/c where I live, this is a time to get rid of it. The question is what I should do to remove it properly. There is no pressure in the system already so it won't be a problem. <BR>Can I plug a/c hoses after disconnecting them from compressor?<BR>What to do with electrical harness off compressor? Can I just leave it as is?<BR>If I turn on a/c without compressor, will cooling fan still kick on?<BR>Anything else that I'm missing?<P>Thank you guys<P>85 GT V6 Auto<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||David Gamache||01-31-2001||10:34 PM||||You could just disconnect the hose connection near the firewall and remove the compressor and its hose. The open hose connections could be pluged if you want to but it is not a big deal.  If dirt gets in the open line it probably won't matter much since the system would have to be flushed before the system, if you decide to repair it later, could be put back into use anyway. As for the wires just zip tie them out of the way and everything else, like your fan should work fine. ||||reg||1
